  "query_preview": "..\n\nDeep upper level troughing located over Washington and Oregon \nwill continue slowly drift eastward over the coming days, which \nwill bring a prolonged period of unsettled weather to the Treasure\nState. Overnight radar has continued to show some showers and \nthunderstorms drifting from SW to NE in two areas: the first \nstretching from around Missoula to Cut Bank, and the second along \nand east of a line from Lewistown to Havre. These two areas will \nlikely continue to persist through late this morning, before a \nbreak arrives for early afternoon. A second, stronger round of \nshowers and thunderstorms will push through the area, with some of\nthe storms becoming strong to severe (more on this below). \n\nAnother round of precipitation will arrive on Monday as a mix of \nconvective and stratiform precipitation, which will generally hang\nacross the plains through Monday night and into Tuesday morning. \nThe convective elements within this precipitation will result in \nsome rather impressive rainfall totals, with most areas across the\nplains likely to see at least 1 to 2 inches through early \nTuesday, with isolated to scattered amounts in excess of 3 or 4 \ninches along and west of Interstate 15 possible where some of the \nheavier convection repeatedly moves over the same areas. While \nflooding is not expected to be a major concern given the overall \ngradual nature of the rainfall, some field/poor drainage flooding \ncannot be ruled out.\n\nWeak upper level ridging will return to the area Wednesday and \nThursday, bringing a reprieve from the rainfall for a brief \nperiod, but another, albeit weaker and faster moving, trough will \nswing through Friday and Saturday, bringing another round of \nwidespread rainfall.\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nThunderstorms today: A potent vort max embedded within the upper \nlevel flow will push into Southwestern Montana late this \nafternoon, kicking off a few showers and thunderstorms as it does \nso. As these storms reach the plains, they will encounter one of \ntwo environments: The first is a heavily capped environment, \nalbeit one with still enough shear to allow the storms to remain \norganized and produce some hail as they move northeastward. The \nsecond possibility is that these storms will encounter a weakly \ncapped atmosphere, which would allow the storms to tap into \nstronger shear and have the potential to become surfaced based, \nallowing both wind and hail to reach the ground as the storms \nevolve into supercells. The Storm Prediction Center has placed \nmost of the area in a slight risk for severe thunderstorms, \nprimarily due to the threat of large hail. \n\nHeavy rainfall through Tuesday: Heavy rainfall through Tuesday\nwill result in a widespread 1 to 2 inches of rainfall across the\nplains, with around a 10% chance of seeing over 4 inches along and\nwest of Interstate 15 north of Great Falls. So far, soils in this\narea seem to have readily accepted the copious amounts of\nrainfall, as areas along the Rocky Mountain Front that have\nreceived over an inch of rainfall over the past 24-36 hours have\nnot really seen rivers respond in any meaningful way. This gives\nhope that soils will continue to be receptive to precipitation,\nthough as soils continue to absorb rainfall it is possible that\nthe soils will saturate and result in some field and poor drainage\nareas, along with some rises on rivers and streams. A Flood Watch\nwas considered, and may still be considered in future updates, but\nfor now soils appear to be capable of handling this long duration\nrainfall event.",
